// The Appeal, ch7: Same Shape. Thursday, April 15 to Saturday, April 17, 2027 (Federal on Thursday night; the Sterling lot, the visiting room and the drive home on Saturday).
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR14x3 LR15x3 LR16 LR06 RC10x2 RC14x2; quiz LR14x3 LR15x4 LR16 LR06 LR04 LR07 LR18 RC10x2 RC14x2 RC08 RC06.
// Full-length passage (p2): a comparative pair on flying in a V. Passage A, the 2001 pelican heart rate study and the 2014 northern bald ibis logger study; Passage B, a caution about reasoning from human raised birds behind an aircraft to wild flocks. Science, written fresh.
// Short case passage (p1): the card Dani is handed at the Sterling visiting desk. The card is fiction; its rules paraphrase CDOC AR 300-01, Offender Visiting Program (effective 05/01/26) and the CDOC visitation rules page.
// Facts checked 2026-10-17 (cdoc.colorado.gov blocks direct fetches; read from Wayback copies and the AR 300-01 PDF linked from the Department Policies page): Sterling Correctional Facility is about 120 miles northeast of Denver; contact visits are requested by email no more than seven days ahead and no later than 8 AM the day before, first come first served; a visitor needs an approved application and photo ID. AR 300-01: one debit or credit card for the vending machines; no phones, watches or books in the visiting area; a short embrace at the start and end and hand holding allowed; nothing passes between visitor and resident except vending machine items; food and drink stay on the side of the table of the person having them, no shared containers; table tops cleared of all checked out items 30 minutes before visiting ends; no hats, hoods, jackets or outerwear in the visiting area; no solid gray, green, orange, white or yellow clothing. The chess set checked out at the desk is inferred from the checked out items rule; CDOC doesn't name games. The 3:30 end matches the West visiting room hours posted in 2025.
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-17: Saturday, April 10; Wednesday, April 14; Thursday, April 15; Saturday, April 17, 2027 (eight weeks to the day before Saturday, June 12). Monday, June 9, 2008.
// Reveal schedule: Luis confirms the first punch ("he put his hands on a kid"), calls the kid nobody three ways counting ch6, and still says he went home alone (f7s8 to f7s12). The bishop on Dani's knight's square is paid off on the real board (f7s11). Ronnie's beer and a Sprite comes after Luis says kid (closing). Visiting starts 9:30 (West room hours posted 2025), so ten to eight leaves an hour and forty minutes. "Who was the kid?" goes on the back of the envelope (closing). Kept off the page per the bible's ch5 and ch6 calls: the truck's departure minute, the grandmother refrain, Dani's Sunday memory, and the ch6 truths.
// Flags set here: pushedluis (f7s10). Read here: promisedhim (f7s8), pushedluis (f7s11, f7s12), overclaimed (f7s11), pushedteo (closing).
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 7, part: `Part Two: The Witnesses`, title: `Same Shape`, weeks: `April 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR04`,`LR06`,`LR07`,`LR14`,`LR15`,`LR16`,`LR18`,`RC06`,`RC08`,`RC10`,`RC14`],
  cast: [
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 61. Sterling Correctional Facility, about two hours northeast. Teaches chess and tutors men for the GED.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Filed the motion in March. Isn't coming to Sterling, on purpose.`],
    [`Ronnie Galvan`,`Bartender at the Night Owl Tap in 2007. Runs a taqueria in Commerce City now.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 30. Hasn't visited their father since 2016.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Thursday, April 15. Ana had cleared the whole desk for once. No freezer bag, no blue copy, no motion. Two pages from Phil's trial binder lay side by side under the lamp, and Dicta lay under the desk with her chin on Ana's shoe.`,
    `Saturday the week before, at six in the morning, Dani had sent an email to an address at Sterling Correctional Facility asking for a contact visit on April 17. Six, because that was the first morning they'd take a request for that date, and the slots went to whoever asked first. The answer came Monday. Approved. Bring your photo ID.`,
    `She hadn't been up since July, a hot Saturday, before any of this. They'd played on a board from the desk that day, a cardboard one with a corner gone soft, and talked about the Rockies the whole time, and she'd driven home with the radio loud.`,
    `Eight weeks to June. When she told him on Wednesday that she was coming, the line went quiet. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Don't wear a jacket. They make you leave it outside."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You're not his lawyer on Saturday,"</span> Ana said, not looking up. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I'm his lawyer. You're his daughter. Don't interview him. Visit him."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"And if he says something?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then you heard it. And you drive home and call me."</span>`,
    `She picked up the pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tonight, shapes."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: same shape`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Bones`,
        body: [`April on Federal, and somebody downstairs is washing a load of horse blankets, from the smell. Dicta is offended. Pencil.`,
          `You've spent since October taking arguments apart. Tonight you take two apart at once and ask whether they're built the same. A parallel reasoning question gives you an argument and five more, on five other subjects, and asks which one reasons the way the first one does. The subject doesn't matter. Foxes, folders, bridges, bread. What matters is the bones.`,
          `So you strip it. Every claim gets a letter and loses its topic. The tracks show a limp; the fox that made them is one of two; only one of the two limps; so it was that one. Bones: X has a trait, X belongs to a small group, only one member of the group has the trait, so X is that member. Write the bones in the margin before you read a single choice. If you can't write them, you haven't read the argument yet.`,
          `Then start from the conclusion, because that's where the quick eliminations are. Look at what kind of claim it is. A must, a probably, a should, a some, a never. If the original concludes that something should be done, an answer that concludes something will happen is gone, however close the rest looks. If the original says probably, an answer that says certainly is gone. Match the conclusion's type and its strength first, then check the premises.`,
          `Order doesn't matter. An argument can put its conclusion first and its rule last and still be the same argument. Two answers will often use the same pieces as the original and arrange them so they run backward, or drop one piece, or soften an all to a most. Those are the ones that feel right and aren't.`,
          `One more thing, and it's the thing people forget. Same shape says nothing about true. A good shape can carry false premises all day, and a bad shape can land on a true conclusion by luck. This question doesn't ask you to judge anything. It asks you to match.`],
        terms: [[`Parallel reasoning`,`An argument with the same structure as the original: the same kind of premises, put together the same way, reaching the same kind of conclusion.`],[`Bones`,`The argument with its subject stripped out, written as letters or plain parts, so you can compare structure.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which argument is most similar in its reasoning to the one above, or which one exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to it. Write the bones first, match the conclusion's type and strength, and only then check the premises.` },
      { h: `Same mistake`,
        body: [`The cousin of that question tells you the original argument is flawed and asks which answer is flawed the same way. Parallel flaw. Here you name the mistake before you look for its twin. Treats a requirement as a guarantee. Takes a trait lots of people have as proof it's one person. Says nobody found it, so it isn't there, when nobody looked.`,
          `Name it in plain words, then go through the choices asking one thing: does this one make that mistake? Not a mistake. That one. An argument with a different flaw is wrong, and so is an argument with no flaw at all. That second trap gets people every time. A choice that reasons correctly can look almost exactly like the original, with one extra word, an only or a probably, that quietly fixes it. Fixed isn't parallel.`,
          `If two choices both seem to make the mistake, go back to the bones and compare the shape of the claims too. The right answer usually matches both the flaw and the structure around it.`],
        terms: [[`Parallel flaw`,`An argument that makes the same logical mistake as the original, usually with a similar structure.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which argument's flawed reasoning is most similar to the reasoning above, or which exhibits a flaw most like the original's. Name the flaw in plain words before you read the choices.` },
      { h: `How, not what`,
        body: [`A method question doesn't ask what someone concluded. It asks how they got there, or how they answered somebody else. The right answer describes the move in general words. Offers a counterexample. Points to a similar case. Questions the source. Rules out the other possibility. Turns to a different issue.`,
          `Read every word of the description against the argument. These answers fail on one word. If the choice says the speaker attacks the other person's character, and the speaker only questions a fact, it's wrong. If it says two cases, and there's one, it's wrong. A description has to be true of the argument all the way through.`,
          `People are slipperier than arguments, by the way. When somebody doesn't want to answer you, they rarely refuse. They answer a different question, one that sounds near enough, often one about you. Notice when the subject changes, and say what it changed to.`],
        terms: [[`Method of reasoning`,`How an argument or a reply works, described in general terms rather than by its content.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask how the argument proceeds, which describes the technique of reasoning, or how one speaker responds to another. Test each word of a choice against what the speaker actually did.` },
      { h: `Two passages`,
        body: [`Once per Reading Comprehension section, usually, you get two shorter passages on one subject instead of one long one. Read the first the ordinary way: main point, attitude, the job each paragraph does. Then read the second asking one question the whole way through. What does this author do to the first one?`,
          `There are only a few answers. Agrees and adds something. Agrees with the facts and disputes what they mean. Applies the first one's idea to a new case. Rejects it outright, which is rarer than people expect. Most second passages accept a good deal and push on one point. Find the point.`,
          `The questions ask about both passages, or one, or how they relate. When a question asks what both authors would accept, it's the yes and yes test from March. Each author has to be committed to it, from what's on the page.`],
        terms: [[`Comparative passages`,`Two passages on one subject, with questions about each and about how they relate.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which statement both authors would most likely agree with, how the second passage relates to the first, or how one author would respond to the other. Decide the relationship in a sentence before you start the questions.` },
      { h: `Most like`,
        body: [`The last one is the parallel question in Reading Comprehension clothes. A passage describes a situation, and the question asks which of five others is most like it. Strip it the same way. Who did what, to whom, for what reason, with what result. Then find the choice with the same relationships, whatever the subject.`,
          `The trap is a choice that shares the subject and not the relationship. If the passage is about a scientist who reports a careful finding and somebody else stretches it, the wrong answer is the other scientist story. The right one might be about a bakery.`],
        terms: [[`Analogy question`,`A question asking which situation is most similar to one in the passage, judged by the relationships involved, not the topic.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which situation is most analogous to the one described, or which is most similar to the relationship between two things in the passage. Name the relationship in plain words, then ignore every choice's topic.` }
    ],
    video: [`iWduWLNTFBk`,`Understanding Parallel Reasoning Questions`,`LSAT Demon`,`7:54`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f7s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR14`, title:`Bones`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana had written the State's case on an index card the way she wrote everything worth keeping, in capitals, in pencil, with no adjectives anywhere.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Ana's card, the State's bones">THE STATE, BONES ONLY.<br>1. THE MAN AT THE CURB WORE A RED JACKET.<br>2. HE WAS A MAN TRAVIS FOUGHT IN THE BAR. (THE GUY FROM THE BAR.)<br>3. OF THE MEN IN THAT FIGHT, ONLY LUIS WORE ONE.<br>SO: THE MAN AT THE CURB WAS LUIS.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Look at it like it's a stranger's,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"If all three lines are true, the last one has to be. That's a good shape. It's what makes it dangerous."</span> She laid four more cards in a row under it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"One of these has the same bones. Ignore the foxes."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tracks by Mrs. Haugen's coop show a limp. The only fox in the county that limps is the old one from the north woods. So he got her hens.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Close,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Count the lines. Ours has three before the so. That one has two. It never says the fox that got her hens is one of the county's. It just leans on that."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. It reaches the same kind of conclusion, but it skips the group step. Ours puts the attacker among the men Travis fought inside, then says only one of them wore red. This one says only one fox in the county limps and never says the fox that got the hens is one of them.`},
        {t:`Tracks by Mrs. Haugen's coop show a limp. The old fox out of the north woods limps too. So the old fox got her hens last night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one they used in the room,"</span> Ana says, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"not the one they wrote down. Hang on to it, because it's the next card."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. Without an only, or a group, nothing ties the tracks to the old fox. For all the card says, half the foxes in Adams County limp.`},
        {t:`Whatever got Mrs. Haugen's hens was one of the two north woods foxes. Of those two, only the old one limps. The tracks limp. So it was him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Same bones,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Out of order, which is allowed. A trait, a small group, one member with the trait. Every line true, the last one has to be."</span> She taps line two on her own card, the guy from the bar. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You don't go after the shape, then. You go after a line."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. Strip the State's card and you get a man with a trait, a small group he belongs to, and only one person in that group with the trait, so he's that person. The fox card has all four, shuffled. Shuffling is allowed. Topic doesn't count. Structure does.`},
        {t:`Whatever got Mrs. Haugen's hens was one of the two north woods foxes. The old one limps. The young one's a lot quicker. So it was the old one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Faster,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Who cares if he's faster? Nothing ties the limp to the coop. It's got our nouns and none of our joints."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. The group of two is there, but whatever took the hens is never said to limp. So the limp isn't tied to anything, and you've got the State's nouns without its structure.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR15`, title:`The one in the room`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana turned the card over. On the back, smaller: MAN AT THE CURB WORE RED. LUIS WORE RED. SO LUIS WAS THE MAN AT THE CURB.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one the jury heard,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Whatever the closing said out loud, that's what twelve people carried into the room. It's broken. You know how. Now find me its twin. Same mistake, nothing added and nothing fixed."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"The woman who took the umbrella wore a green raincoat. Inez had one on that day. So Inez could be the one who took it."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Could be,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's true. That's the whole trouble with it. Could be isn't the mistake. Could be is the fix, not the mistake."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Same pieces, but "could be" is a conclusion the premises support. It doesn't make the mistake, so it can't be the twin of an argument that does.`},
        {t:`"The woman who took the umbrella wore a green raincoat. Inez had one on that day. So Inez took the umbrella."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Twins,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A thing plenty of people have, treated like a thing one person has. Green raincoat, red jacket. Nobody counted either."</span> She writes COUNT on the back of the card and underlines it.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. A trait the culprit had, which the suspect also has, treated as proof the suspect is the culprit. Lots of women own green raincoats, and lots of men wore red that night. Same mistake, same size.`},
        {t:`"Only one woman in the lobby wore a green raincoat, and the woman who took the umbrella wore one. Inez did. So Inez took it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Only,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Put only in and it works. That's the front of the card, not the back. You found the one they wrote down."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. The word "only" repairs the reasoning. With it, the conclusion follows, so there's no flaw to match.`},
        {t:`"Nobody saw any woman but Inez near the umbrella rack that whole day. So Inez is the one who took the umbrella from it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Different hole,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's nobody saw anybody else. A real mistake. Not this one."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. This argues from no one else being seen, which is a different mistake. Nothing in it treats a common trait as unique.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC14`, title:`Two closings`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Then the two pages under the lamp. The prosecution's closing on the left, the one Dani had read in October with the binder's rubber band breaking in her hand. On the right, a page she'd never seen.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Monday, June 9, 2008, the prosecution's closing argument">Consuelo Baca never saw a face. She told you that herself, and you can trust a witness who tells you what she didn't see. What she saw was a man in a red jacket strike Travis Hensley at a quarter past eleven and run. So who wore a red jacket that night? The defendant did. His own daughter told you he wore it to work that day. Ronnie Galvan told you the defendant and Travis fought inside the bar less than an hour before. The lab told you Travis Hensley's blood was on the cuff. And the next morning, before any officer knocked, that jacket was in the washer, in a house where he never did the laundry. The defendant told police he never touched anyone. The blood on his cuff says he did. People don't wash what they aren't worried about. Put it together, and the man in the red jacket was Luis Ruiz.</prop>`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Monday, June 9, 2008, the defense closing argument">Mrs. Baca told you the truth. She saw a man in a red jacket, and she never saw his face. So ask yourselves how many red jackets were in that bar. Luis Ruiz works at Platte Valley Refining, and so does every man on his crew, and every one of them wears that jacket. His daughter told you so. Mr. Galvan says there was a scuffle inside, and men bleed in scuffles. Blood on a cuff tells you there was a bar. It doesn't tell you there was a parking lot. A work jacket went in the washer on a Sunday, which is where work jackets go. The State had to show you it was this man, in this jacket, at that curb. It showed you a jacket.</prop>`,
        `Ana slid Phil's page over next to the other one so the edges lined up. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil's,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read them as a pair, the way you'd read two witnesses to the same thing. What does the second one do to the first?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It names another man from the refinery crew and argues he's the one who struck Travis.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find the name,"</span> Ana says. There isn't one. Phil said every man on the crew, and stopped there.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Not in the passage. The defense closing points to the many crew jackets but never names or accuses anyone.`},
        {t:`It uses Consuelo's own words to argue that the man at the curb couldn't have been Luis.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Couldn't,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He never says couldn't. He says the State didn't show it. Those are different closings, and he gave the smaller one."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Half right. Phil does start from Consuelo's testimony. But his conclusion is that the State didn't prove it was Luis, not that it couldn't have been.`},
        {t:`It grants the State's main facts and says they fit other men, or innocent reasons.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Every one,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The jacket, yes, twenty jackets. The blood, yes, from a bar. The washer, yes, it's a work jacket. He took their pieces one at a time and showed the jury each one fit more than one picture."</span> She runs the pencil down Phil's page. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not bad. Not enough."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Phil grants the jacket, the blood and the washer and gives each another explanation. He doesn't deny the State's facts. He disputes what they show. That's the relationship.`},
        {t:`It disputes the State's facts one by one and says its witnesses were wrong about them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read his first sentence again,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Mrs. Baca told you the truth. He believed every witness the State had. What he didn't believe was the arithmetic."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Contradicted. Phil says Consuelo told the truth and builds on Dani's and Ronnie's testimony. He accepts their facts.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC10`, title:`A white van`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana put her finger on the prosecution's first line. Consuelo Baca never saw a face.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She gave him a color. He gave the jury a man. That's what he did with her, start to finish."</span> Another row of cards. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find me that somewhere else. Not a courtroom."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`A neighbor reports a white van at the empty house but never saw the driver. The landlord finds out the tenant's cousin drives one and bills him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's her,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She said exactly what she saw and nothing more. The landlord did the rest and used her name to do it."</span> Dani thinks of the confession booth, every Saturday for a year.`,
          why:`Analogy. In the closing, a witness's careful, limited account, a color and no face, is joined to other facts and used for an identification she never made. The neighbor and the landlord have the same relationship.`},
        {t:`A neighbor reports a white van at the empty house and names the driver, then later admits she only guessed at who was behind the wheel.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Consuelo never guessed,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She told them twice she didn't see a face. This woman's the problem. Consuelo wasn't."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. The witness here overstated and took it back. Consuelo never claimed to know who it was. The fault in the closing is what someone else did with her words.`},
        {t:`A neighbor reports a white van and a clear look at the driver's face, and the landlord, checking her story, finds the tenant's cousin.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A clear look,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then she identified him. Consuelo never could. That's the one fact the whole closing had to talk its way around."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. It has a witness and someone acting on her report. But this witness saw a face, which is the very thing Consuelo lacked, so the relationship is different.`},
        {t:`A neighbor who saw nothing at all that night is quoted by the landlord as having seen a white van parked at the empty house.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He didn't make her up,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Worse. He quoted her right."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. This is a made up report. The prosecutor quoted Consuelo accurately. What he added was the man.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s5`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC14`, title:`Neither one`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Dani read the two pages again, the left and then the right. Two lawyers, two hours apart on the same Monday, going opposite ways.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now what they agree on without saying it,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Both of them stood up and took something for granted. What?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the jacket Consuelo saw was a Platte Valley work jacket, the same as the one Luis wore.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The prosecutor does,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil only says there were a lot of them. He never says the one at the curb was one. Two yeses or it's out."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. The prosecution's closing treats it as Luis's jacket. Phil talks about how many crew jackets there were but never commits to the one Consuelo saw being one of them.`},
        {t:`That the man Consuelo saw at the curb was wearing a ball cap as well as the red jacket.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Neither page says cap. Not once. She'd read the whole line in December, and here were two lawyers in 2008, both of them looking right at a man in a red jacket and nothing else.`,
          why:`Comparative passages. Neither closing mentions a cap. You can't agree on something neither of you said or implied.`},
        {t:`That the man at the curb came out of that bar, so the only question was which of its men.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That bar,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The prosecutor goes straight from the curb to the fight inside. Phil says how many jackets were in that bar. Neither one ever looks outside it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"And neither of them says cap."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil argued their case with better manners."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. The prosecutor goes from the curb straight to the fight inside, and Phil counts the jackets in that bar. Both of them are treating the attacker as one of the customers. Neither one ever says it out loud.`},
        {t:`That Luis never laid a hand on Travis Hensley at any point that night, inside the bar or out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The prosecutor says the blood on the cuff shows he did,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil says men bleed in scuffles. No one stood up there in 2008 and defended never."</span>`,
          why:`Comparative passages. The prosecution's closing says the blood proves he touched Travis, and Phil explains the blood by a scuffle. Neither accepts the claim.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s6`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC10`, title:`In his hand`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana tapped the dispatch log in its sleeve. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So Phil had this,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tabbed. Highlighted, half of it. And he stood up in front of that jury and argued the face, and the jackets, and the washer, and he never said cap."</span> She looked at his page a while. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What's that like?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`A student contesting a grade shows the teacher's own email admitting a scoring error, and the grade gets changed that afternoon.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She used it,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil didn't. That's the whole difference, and it's the one that matters."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. Here the decisive document gets used. In the closing it sat in a folder.`},
        {t:`A student contesting a grade invents an email from the teacher admitting an error, and argues from the email.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The log was real,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That was the trouble with it."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. Inventing evidence is the opposite of what happened. Phil had real evidence and didn't use it.`},
        {t:`A student contesting a grade has no proof of any error at all, and argues that the test ran long and was unfair.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No proof,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil had proof. Arguing the weak points is the same. Having the strong one in your hand isn't."</span>`,
          why:`Analogy. The argument is similar, side points instead of the main one, but this student has nothing better. Phil did, and that's the relationship the question is about.`},
        {t:`A student contesting a grade has the teacher's email admitting a scoring error, and argues only that the test ran long.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's him,"</span> Ana says. Then, quieter, to the page and not to Dani: <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I'd know it anywhere."</span> She doesn't say why. Dani thinks of a brief with nine issues, and the jacket ninth, and doesn't say anything either.`,
          why:`Analogy. The student holds the decisive document and argues weaker points instead. Phil had the dispatch log with the cap in it and argued the face, the jackets and the washer. Same relationship.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s7`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR15`, title:`Not developed`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, April 17. She left at a quarter to six in the dark, up I-76 with the heater on and the radio off, and somewhere past Hudson the sky went gray and then pink over the fields. Feedlots. A grain elevator with a town's name painted on it. Long stretches with nothing on either side but wind.`,
        `She was early. She was always early. At ten to eight she was parked in the visitors' lot with the engine off, looking at two lines of fence and a low tan building, and she had an hour and forty minutes.`,
        `Ana had sent her a photo the night before, without a word. The last paragraph of Gene's 2007 summary, typed on a machine with a sticky e.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Detective G. Vigil, case summary, October 29, 2007, last paragraph">Witnesses at the scene did not report any other male in a confrontation with the victim. No other suspect was developed.</prop>`,
        `She knew what sat under that paragraph, because she'd read it all. Patrons' names not obtained. The bar empty before the first car came. A refinery crew list Gene got from the shift supervisor and never called. She wrote it out on the inside cover of her prep book the way Ana would: nobody reported another man; nobody asked; so there wasn't one. Then she went looking for its twin.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`No guests reported any noise on the fourth floor, so the fourth floor has none. The hotel only asked guests on the first three floors.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Same hole. You can't report what nobody asks you about. She circles it, and then she circles did not report in Gene's paragraph, on her phone, with her thumb, which does nothing at all.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. No report, from people who were never asked, taken as proof there's nothing to report. The hotel never asked the fourth floor, and nobody asked the crew. Same mistake.`},
        {t:`No guests reported any noise on the fourth floor, so there probably isn't much. Every guest on that floor was asked about it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Every guest was asked. Then silence means something. That's what Gene's paragraph would say if somebody had done the work.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Close in subject and shape, but asking everyone repairs the reasoning, and "probably" keeps it modest. There's no flaw left to match.`},
        {t:`All the guests on the fourth floor complained about noise, so every floor in the hotel must have a noise problem.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`From one floor to all of them. A real mistake. Gene never made it. He made the opposite one, from nobody to nothing.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. This generalizes from one part to the whole. Gene's mistake is treating an unasked silence as an answer.`},
        {t:`The manager says there's no noise problem on the fourth floor, and the manager is never wrong, so there isn't one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Taking somebody's word because they're the one saying it. Gene's paragraph doesn't do that. It says nobody said anything.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. This rests on an authority being trusted. Gene's reasoning rests on the absence of reports, a different flaw.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR16`, title:`Nobody`, q:`What did Pa just do with her question?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Her phone went in the glove box with the prep book and the envelope. Her jacket stayed on the seat. Inside, at the desk, she showed her license and the one debit card they let you bring for the machines, and a woman in a fleece vest handed her a laminated card to read while she waited.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Card at the visiting desk">Bring one photo ID and one debit or credit card. No phones, watches, books or papers past this desk. Coats, jackets, hats and hoods stay outside. Except for vending machine items, nothing passes between visitor and resident. Games go back to the desk thirty minutes before visiting ends.</prop>`,
        `It was cold in there, like he'd said, and bright. Round tables bolted down, the machines on one wall, a TV up in a corner with the sound off showing a golf tournament nobody was watching. He came through the far door in the green they all wore. Older than his voice. Grayer, thinner through the shoulders. Then he saw her and grinned and walked too fast, and the officer said something, and he slowed down about one step's worth.`,
        ((s.flags.promisedhim||G.promisedhim)
          ?`They got the short hug the card allowed. Into her hair he said, <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You here to get me out?"</span> She didn't answer that.`
          :`They got the short hug the card allowed. Into her hair he said, <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You got tall."</span> She'd been this tall since she was fourteen.`),
        `Two coffees from the machine, on her debit card. His stayed on his side of the table. Rules. The woman at the desk had a cardboard chess set in a box with a rubber band on it, and Dani signed for it on a clipboard with a pen on a string. No notes. He set up his side and she set up hers, and when he got to the bishop he put it on her knight's square without looking up. She let him.`,
        `Lupe's crown for a while. The glitter glue. How Lupe wore it herself in the end. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your brother?"</span> Thirty, she said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I know how old he is."</span>`,
        `She'd driven two hours to ask it to his face. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Pa. Ronnie says you swung first."</span>`,
        `He looked at the board a long time. On the phone that one always came back fast, before she finished asking. Not today. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I hit him first."</span> He didn't say anything else for a while. Then, to the bishop: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He put his hands on a kid."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"What kid?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Some kid. Nobody."</span> He picked up his coffee and put it down without drinking any. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You look tired, mija. Eight weeks out from your test and you drive up here in the dark. You eating? Brennan has you up till midnight, I bet. You should be home with your book."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"You're trying to make me feel bad for driving all the way up here today, Pa."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I'm glad you came,"</span> he says, and means it, and that's the trouble with her answer. He isn't scolding her. He's taking care of her, and it works just as well.`,
          why:`Method of reasoning. It notices that he's talking about her, which is right. But it names a motive, guilt, that he never shows. Describe what he did, not what you guess he meant.`},
        {t:`"You're saying the kid doesn't matter because nothing bad ever happened to him."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I didn't say anything happened or didn't,"</span> he says. True. He said nobody. She'd filled in the rest.`,
          why:`Method of reasoning. He makes no claim about what happened to the kid. A description of the move has to be true of what he said.`},
        {t:`"I asked who the kid was, Pa, and you're asking if I'm eating."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`He looks at her a long moment, and then he almost smiles. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Eat something on the way home,"</span> he says, which is the same move again, and they both hear it.`,
          why:`Method of reasoning. She asked about the kid, and he turned to her test, her sleep and her eating. He didn't refuse. He changed the subject to what she needs. That's the move.`},
        {t:`"You're saying Ronnie got it wrong and you never swung at that man first."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I just told you I did,"</span> he says, quietly, and she hears it again, the thing she drove up here for. She'd answered the man on the phone, not the one at the table.`,
          why:`Method of reasoning. Contradicted. He's just admitted the first punch, at this table, out loud, after denying it to Gene in 2007 and to her on the phone in January. So a description of his move can't have him denying it. The move she has to describe comes afterward, when she asks him about the kid and he starts talking about her sleep and her test instead.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR14`, title:`Going home`, q:`Which line about Benny has the same shape as Pa's argument about going home?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"And after?"</span> she said.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"After, Ronnie threw me out, and I went home. By myself."</span> He said the going home part to the board, in the same words he'd given Gene in 2007 and her on a hundred Wednesdays since. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"A man who just got thrown out of a bar for fighting goes home, mija. He doesn't go back out looking for more. I got thrown out. So I went home."</span>`,
        `At the next table a little boy in light up shoes was trying to climb into his father's lap, and his mother kept telling him not yet, not yet, baby, and the shoes kept flashing red against the table leg. Dani watched them for a second. Then she answered her father the way he'd taught her to answer a move on the garage sale board, with a move of her own.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Benny, then. Most players who lose their queen resign, so Benny probably did when he lost his."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Probably,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I didn't say probably, mija."</span> He hadn't. He'd said goes home, flat, as if it were printed in a rule book somewhere and he was reading it off the page.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. The pieces match, but most and probably make it a softer argument than his. His rule says what a man in that spot does, and his conclusion has no probably in it.`},
        {t:`"Benny, then. A player who just lost his queen resigns. Benny just lost his queen. So Benny resigned."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`He laughs, the real laugh, and the woman at the desk looks up from her clipboard. Benny never resigns, he says. Benny plays every game out to the last pawn and then wants to shake on it. When he's finished he asks if that makes it a bad argument, and she says no, it makes it a shape, and a shape can't tell you if something is true. He asks what does, then. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Somebody who checks,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Like Brennan,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Like me."</span> He laughs again, shorter, and looks at the bishop.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. He takes a rule about anybody in a situation and applies it to himself: thrown out men go home, he got thrown out, so he went home. Benny's argument is built the same way. Matching the shape doesn't tell you anything about whether it's true. Benny, who has never resigned a game in his life, is how a rule like that fails.`},
        {t:`"Benny, then. He resigned, so he must have lost his queen to somebody right before it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's backward,"</span> he says, the way he said it when she was eight and pushed pawns backward to see what he'd do about it. He was right then, too.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. This one's reversed. It runs from the result back to the situation, and that's a different shape. It's also a broken one, since a man can resign for a lot of reasons.`},
        {t:`"Benny, then. Nobody ever saw Benny keep playing, so I guess Benny quit the game."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I didn't say nobody saw me,"</span> he says, which is true. He'd given her a rule about what men do. Who was watching never came into it.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. That one argues from nobody seeing something happen. His argument rests on a general rule about men who get thrown out, applied to his own case.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR15`, title:`One thing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`It was right there. He'd told Gene in 2007 he never touched anybody. He'd told her the same thing on the phone in January, fast, before she'd even finished asking. Now he'd told her, across a cardboard board, that it wasn't true. So the rest of it was a lie too. Going home was a lie. The whole thing. That step was easy. Too easy. She'd missed the bottom stair at home that way a hundred times, in the dark, going down for the laundry.`,
        `He was watching her face. He'd been reading it since she was eight. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Go on,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Say it. You left the house before six for this."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"I won't say it, Pa. It'd be like burning one piece of toast and deciding every piece on the plate's burned."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`He doesn't answer right away. At the next table the boy has made it into his father's lap at last, and the mother is reading the vending machine prices out loud to no one in particular. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You're not going to say it,"</span> her father says. She says no. He says okay. It's what he used to say at the kitchen table when she took a move back and he let her. His hand goes around his paper cup on his side of the table and stays there.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Her thought would jump from one proven lie to everything he's said, the same jump as from one burned slice of toast to the whole plate. One false claim doesn't show the others are false.`},
        {t:`"I won't say it. It'd be like the cook burning the toast once, so he'll probably burn the eggs too."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Probably,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So that's what you think, mija. Probably."</span> She didn't mean it like that. She'd meant to soften it, and said out loud it went the same direction, just slower.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. Close, but it softens the jump. The thought she's fighting goes from one lie to all of it, with no probably anywhere. A twin has to make the same size of mistake.`},
        {t:`"You lied to Gene about the punch, Pa. So you lied about going home too. Where'd you really go?"`, s:0, flag:`pushedluis`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I went home,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Go write that on Brennan's board."</span> After that he looks at the pieces and not at her. He touches his own king and lets go of it. When the boy at the next table starts crying he flinches, a real one, and she sees it and can't say what she saw.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. This one doesn't match the mistake. It is the mistake. One proven lie doesn't show the rest is a lie, and asking it this way, here, pushes him past anything he'll give.`},
        {t:`"I won't say it, Pa. It'd be like saying the cook never burns anything, so he couldn't have burned this toast."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's what I told Gene,"</span> he says, dry, and drinks some of his coffee. It was. Never, so not this time. That mistake was his, not hers, and he'd been making it since 2007.`,
          why:`Parallel flaw. A different flaw: from never to not this once. The thought she's fighting runs from one lie to every claim.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR14`, title:`The board`, q:`Which spill story most closely parallels Brennan's argument?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At three the woman at the desk called it. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Games back, folks. Thirty minutes."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's been sitting there since January,"</span> her father said, about the bishop.`,
        `It was still on her knight's old square. Two of her pawns could take it. She went for the one next to her king, the one Lupe had poked at in January, and stopped with her hand over it.`,
        `She'd never once looked at the whole board on the basement step. She looked now. If that pawn took, the file in front of her king opened up, and his queen had been sitting at the far end of it for weeks. Teo had said it in January, in her kitchen, zipping Lupe's coat with grease to his elbows: he gives you something easy to take, so you're busy taking it.`,
        `She took with the other pawn.`,
        ((s.flags.pushedluis||G.pushedluis)
          ?`He sat back and didn't look up. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Huh."</span> Then, to the board: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your brother always took with that one. Every time. Ten years old."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I know. He told me you used to give him something easy to take."</span> His hand stopped on the lid of the box and stayed.`
          :`He sat back. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Huh."</span> Then, softer: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your brother always took with that one. Every time. Ten years old."</span> She said she knew. Teo told her, January, at her table. Her father looked at her, then at the board, a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He remembered that,"</span> he said.`),
        `The woman came around for the box. Dani said the position over once in her head, for the board at home, and the pieces went back in the cardboard with the rubber band. Then he wanted Brennan's argument. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Short,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Like I'm Rudy."</span>`,
        ((s.flags.overclaimed||G.overclaimed)
          ?`She thought for a second about her own sentence on a page on Federal, and Ana's two circles around one word. Then she put it in refinery terms. He'd done fourteen years at one.`
          :`She put it in refinery terms. He'd done fourteen years at one.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Say the foreman blames you for a spill, since yours was the only open valve. Three were open. So it wasn't you."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Wasn't me,"</span> he says, slowly, and she hears what she just gave him. Ana never wrote that anywhere. If Ana had seen it she'd have crossed it out twice.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. Too strong. Ana's argument ends with "they didn't show it," not "it wasn't him." Knocking out the State's reason doesn't prove the opposite of what the State concluded.`},
        {t:`"A foreman blames you for a spill because yours was the only valve open. Three were open. So he hasn't shown it was you."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So she's not saying I didn't do it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"She's saying they never showed that you did."</span> He nods, slowly, and turns his cup. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's a smaller thing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It's the whole thing, Pa."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Smart woman,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Rudy would get that. Rudy would want it on a card."</span>`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. Ana's argument: the identification rested on Luis being the only man in red; he wasn't; so the State didn't prove it was him. The valve version has the same parts and the same modest ending, that the reason fails, not that the opposite is true.`},
        {t:`"Say a foreman pins a spill on you because your valve was the only one open. Three were. So it was one of the others."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"One of the other guys,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That what she's saying?"</span> It isn't. Ana isn't saying anybody, and Dani had just swapped the end of the argument out for a different one without noticing she'd done it.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. The setup matches, but the conclusion jumps to another culprit. Ana's motion doesn't name anyone. It says the State's reason fails.`},
        {t:`"Say the foreman blames you for a spill. If your valve was open, you caused it. Your valve was open. So you did."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's the State's,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I sat through that one."</span> He did. Six days of it, in the white shirt her mother took to the jail for him, with Phil Abrams beside him.`,
          why:`Parallel reasoning. This is a different shape, a rule applied straight to his case, and it points the other way. Ana's argument takes a reason away. It doesn't supply one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f7s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, title:`Capable`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By three twenty there was nothing on the table but their two paper cups. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Pa. The kid,"</span> she said, and he said, <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Nobody, mija,"</span> quietly. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Let it be nobody."</span> After that he told her to tell Brennan yes on the swab, that she'd asked in her letter. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"And tell her she wasn't wrong."</span> He'd had that ready. Probably since March.`,
        `She'd had hers ready since December, since the night at the kitchen table with the envelope. Pa, I'm not sure you didn't. She put her hand over his. The card said you could. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Pa, I'm not,"</span> she said. He waited. Somebody's debit card beeped at the machine by the wall and a bag of chips dropped. The kid in the light up shoes was asleep on his dad now with one shoe still going. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I'm not going to make it back up here before the test,"</span> she said, and he said good, study.`,
        ((s.flags.pushedluis||G.pushedluis)
          ?`His hand stayed where it was, under hers, and didn't turn over.`
          :`His hand turned over under hers and stayed there.`),
        ((s.flags.pushedluis||G.pushedluis)
          ?`The hug at the end was as short as the card said. He let go first.`
          :`The hug at the end was supposed to be short, and the officer by the door gave them an extra second anyway and looked at his clipboard.`),
        `Outside the wind had come up hard enough to rock the car on its springs. She got her phone out of the glove box and called Ana before she'd even put her jacket back on. Ana picked up on the first ring and said well. Dani told her. He swung first, because some man had his hands on a kid. Ana didn't say anything. Then she asked what kid, and Dani said he says nobody.`,
        `Ana was quiet long enough for a truck to go by on Federal behind her. When Ana came back she was doing Meredith, flatter and faster, the way she always did it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"All right. He admits he hit Travis Hensley first. So he's a man who'd hit Travis Hensley. A man who'd hit him once in a bar is capable of the rest of it in a parking lot. What's wrong with that?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"He only swung because Travis grabbed a kid, Ana. Anybody would've done the same."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Maybe a jury likes him better for it,"</span> Ana says. Then she tells Dani that's a jury question, and this is a logic question. Dani stood up for the punch, and Meredith never went after the punch. She went after what she says the punch tells you about later.`,
          why:`Flaw. Having a good reason for the punch might make Luis easier to like on a witness stand. It does nothing to the step Meredith takes, from what he did inside the bar to what he must have done outside later.`},
        {t:`"Capable isn't did. Half that bar was capable. She'd still need him at the curb."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the sentence,"</span> Ana says, and tells her to write it down before she starts the car, on anything she's got. Dani finds a napkin in the glove box from a burrito place in Fort Morgan and writes it on her knee in capitals. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Dani,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Drive slow."</span>`,
          why:`Flaw. The argument goes from could have to did. Plenty of people are capable of plenty of things, so capable doesn't pick out this man. Meredith would still have to put him at the curb.`},
        {t:`"A punch in a bar isn't the same thing as hitting a man with something at a curb."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"True,"</span> Ana says. Meredith will just tell the jury one leads to the other, she says. Dani is arguing about how far apart the two are, and that isn't where Meredith is weak. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's the kind of step,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Capable, to did."</span>`,
          why:`Flaw. Pointing to the difference between a punch inside and a blow at the curb helps a little. The bigger problem is still capable to did. That step would be broken even if the two acts were exactly alike.`},
        {t:`"She can't use it. He's only said it to me at a table, never once in court."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I asked you what was wrong with the argument,"</span> Ana says, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"not whether she gets to make it."</span> Someday she might get to, Ana says, and Dani had better have an answer ready when she does.`,
          why:`Flaw. Whether the statement could ever come in at a hearing is a separate question from whether the reasoning works, and the question here is about the reasoning.`}
      ]},
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f7s2`, title:`Match the shape`, intro:`Ana's index cards, in pencil, a little smudged. Each card has an argument somebody actually made, and three sets of bones. Ignore the subject. Which bones belong to it?`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. Father Ruben, setting up for a funeral lunch: "Every folding chair in this hall came from Belmont's. This one's in the hall. So it's a Belmont's chair, and it goes back Monday."`, choices:[`A rule, a case that doesn't meet it, so the opposite happens.`,`All of a group have it, this one's in the group, so it has it.`,`Most of a group have it, this one's in the group, so it probably does.`], answer:1, why:`Every chair, this chair, so this chair. Father Ruben never said most, and nobody's case is failing to meet anything. The chair goes back Monday.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. The man who walks Dicta on Saturdays, about the softball game in the park: "If it rains, they cancel. They didn't cancel. So it didn't rain."`, choices:[`If this, then that. That happened, so this did.`,`If this, then that. That didn't happen, so this didn't.`,`One of two things happened. Not this one, so the other.`], answer:1, why:`No cancel, so no rain. It's the one free move a rule gives you, and he makes it. The first set is the famous mistake, the one where they cancel and you decide it rained, when they cancel for mud too, and for weddings.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. My downstairs neighbor, about the banging: "It's the attic or the garage. There's nothing else on that side of the house. It isn't the garage. So it's the attic."`, choices:[`One of two places. Not this one, so the other one.`,`Usually in one place. Not there today, so the other.`,`Found it in one place, so it isn't in the other.`], answer:0, why:`Two places, not the garage, so the attic. He never said usually, and he hasn't found anything yet. He's just tired of the banging.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. "Some of the jurors were nurses. Some of the jurors were from Thornton. So some of the nurses were from Thornton."`, choices:[`Some are one thing and some are another, so some are both. That doesn't follow.`,`All are one thing and some are another, so some are both. That follows.`,`Some are one thing and all are another, so some are both. That follows.`], answer:0, why:`Two somes don't have to touch. The nurses could all be from Brighton. So the card's bones are the broken set.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. "People who sleep less weigh more. So sleeping less makes you gain weight."`, choices:[`They go together, so one causes the other.`,`It changed where nothing else did, so it probably helped.`,`They show up together, so at least sometimes they show up together.`], answer:0, why:`Two things show up together, and the card says one causes the other. Nobody held anything else steady, and nobody said probably. The third set is true and useless.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. "The snow plan worked in Brighton, and Thornton's like Brighton in every way that matters to the plan. So it'll work in Thornton."`, choices:[`It worked in one place, so it'll work anywhere at all.`,`It worked there, and here is like there where it counts, so it'll work here.`,`It worked in two places, so it'll work everywhere else.`], answer:1, why:`A comparison that says the two cases match where it counts. That middle line is the whole argument. Without that line you're left with the first set. That's how most people pick a diet.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Card handed to visitors at the Sterling visiting desk`,
      text:[`STERLING CORRECTIONAL FACILITY, VISITING ROOM. PLEASE READ BEFORE YOU ENTER.`,
        `Bring one photo ID and one debit or credit card for the vending machines. Nothing else goes past this desk: no phones, no watches, no books or papers.`,
        `Coats, jackets, hats and hooded clothing stay outside the visiting room. Visitors may not wear solid gray, green, orange, white or yellow, because those colors can be mistaken for what residents wear.`,
        `You may share a short embrace at the beginning and end of the visit, and you may hold hands at the table. Except for items bought from the vending machines, nothing may pass between a visitor and a resident. Food and drink stay on the side of the table of the person having them, and no two people share a container.`,
        `Games and other items checked out at this desk must be returned thirty minutes before visiting ends.`,
        `Staff may end a visit for any violation of these rules.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: two passages on flying in a V`,
      text:[`Passage A`,
        `Anyone who has watched geese pass over in the fall has seen the V, and for more than a century people have suspected it saves the birds work. The idea comes from aerodynamics. A flapping wing leaves behind it a swirl of air that rises just beyond the wingtip. A bird flying a little behind and to the side of another could ride that rising air and spend less effort holding itself up. The theory is tidy. Testing it in wild birds is not, since nobody can fit a flock of geese with instruments and fly beside it for a thousand miles.`,
        `Two studies got around that by using birds raised by people. In 2001 researchers working with great white pelicans trained to fly behind a motorboat and a light aircraft recorded the birds' heart rates in flight. Pelicans flying in formation had lower heart rates than pelicans flying alone, and they flapped less often and glided more. In 2014 a team studied northern bald ibises, an endangered species, being taught a migration route from Austria to Italy behind a microlight aircraft. Each bird carried a small logger that recorded its position and every beat of its wings. The ibises tended to fly in the positions theory predicted, and they timed their wingbeats to use the rising air from the bird ahead. A bird flying directly behind another, where the air sinks, shifted its timing the other way.`,
        `Taken together, the two studies turn an old guess into a measured fact. Birds in a V save energy, and they work at it.`,
        `Passage B`,
        `The pelican and ibis studies are careful, and nobody doubts their measurements. What deserves more attention is the setting. Both used birds raised by people and led by a boat or an aircraft, because those are birds researchers can fit with instruments and follow. That is a sound reason to choose them. It is not a reason to assume that a wild flock behaves the same way.`,
        `An aircraft holds a steady speed and a straight line. A wild flock does neither for long. Its members differ in age and strength, it changes leaders, and it adjusts to winds no pilot planned for. Many large wild flocks fly in loose, shifting lines that only now and then form a clean V.`,
        `A formation may also do more than one thing. In a V each bird can keep the others in sight, so a flock can turn together, and young birds can stay near older ones that have made the trip before. None of this contradicts the energy savings. But finding that a formation can save energy is different from finding that saving energy is why wild birds form one, and different again from measuring how much they save on an ordinary day in October. The studies tell us what birds are able to do. How often wild flocks actually do it remains open.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Guild rules require that every apprentice in the Dunmore weavers' guild complete a masterwork, judged by three senior members, before being admitted as a full member. According to the guild's records, the apprentice Pell, though widely admired for her skill, has not yet completed a masterwork. So Pell has not been admitted as a full member of the guild.<br><br>Which one of the following arguments is most similar in its pattern of reasoning to the argument above?`,
      opts:[`Every runner in the Halvard marathon must finish a qualifying race first. Tove has finished a qualifying race. So Tove has entered the Halvard marathon.`,`Every member of the Linden choir must attend Thursday rehearsals. Lina attends Thursday rehearsals. So Lina is a member of the Linden choir.`,`Most players on the Corran team practice daily before a match. Ivo didn't practice daily this week. So Ivo probably won't play in Saturday's match.`,`Any tenant in the Bexley building must sign a lease before getting a key. Omar hasn't signed one. So Omar hasn't gotten a key to the building.`,`No student may enter the chemistry lab without goggles. Rae has brought her own goggles today. So Rae may enter the chemistry lab this afternoon.`],
      a:3, why:`Parallel reasoning. The original says a requirement comes before admission, the requirement isn't met, so admission hasn't happened. The tenant argument has the same structure: no lease, so no key. The runner, choir and lab arguments all treat meeting a requirement as if it were enough. The team argument softens the rule to most and the conclusion to probably.`, task:`LR14`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`The water under the kitchen counter at the Ferro house is coming either from the dishwasher or from the sink drain, since nothing else in that cabinet carries water. The plumber who inspected the dishwasher this morning found its seals intact and both of its hoses completely dry. So the water is coming from the sink drain.<br><br>The pattern of reasoning in which one of the following is most similar to that in the argument above?`,
      opts:[`The missing invoice is in either the blue folder or the red one, the only folders Gil used this week. Gil searched the blue folder and didn't find it. So it's in the red folder.`,`The missing invoice is in either the blue folder or the red one, the only folders Gil used this week. Gil found the invoice in the blue folder. So the invoice is not in the red folder.`,`The missing invoice is probably in the blue folder, since Gil usually keeps his invoices there. Gil hasn't checked the blue folder yet. So the invoice isn't in the red folder.`,`Gil used both the blue folder and the red folder this week, and the invoice he needs is missing. Gil is careful with paper. So Gil must have left the invoice in one of the folders.`,`If the missing invoice were in the red folder, Gil would have found it by now. Gil hasn't searched the red folder at all. So the missing invoice isn't in the red folder.`],
      a:0, why:`Parallel reasoning. The original names two possible sources, rules out one, and concludes it's the other. The first invoice argument does the same. The second runs from finding it in one place to not the other, a different move. The third starts from probably and usually. The fourth never rules anything out. The last is a conditional that doesn't even fit its own facts.`, task:`LR14`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`City engineer: Any bridge that fails two safety inspections in a row should be closed to traffic until it has been repaired. The Corbett Street bridge failed its spring inspection this year, and last month it failed its fall inspection as well. So the Corbett Street bridge should be closed to traffic until it's repaired.<br><br>Which one of the following exhibits a pattern of reasoning most similar to the engineer's?`,
      opts:[`Any restaurant that fails two health inspections in a row loses its license. Nolan's lost its license this spring, right after its June visit. So Nolan's must have failed two health inspections in a row.`,`Any restaurant that fails two health inspections in a row should lose its license. Nolan's failed only one inspection this year, in March. So Nolan's should lose its license.`,`Restaurants that fail two health inspections in a row usually lose their licenses. Nolan's failed two. So Nolan's will probably lose its license soon.`,`Nolan's failed two health inspections in a row, and it lost its license. So any restaurant that fails two inspections should lose its license.`,`A restaurant that fails two health inspections in a row should lose its license till it passes one. Nolan's failed its March and June inspections, back to back. So it should lose its license till it passes.`],
      a:4, why:`Parallel reasoning. A rule says what should happen when a condition is met, the case meets it, so the thing should happen. The last restaurant argument matches, including the should. One runs backward from the result, one applies the rule to a case that doesn't meet it, one predicts instead of recommending, and one builds a rule from a single case.`, task:`LR14`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Pool manager: Anyone hired as a lifeguard at the city pool this summer must first pass the county's swim test, which includes a timed four hundred meter swim. Anya Lund passed the county's swim test last week, with one of the best times anyone has posted this year. So Anya will be hired as a lifeguard at the city pool this summer.<br><br>The flawed reasoning in which one of the following is most similar to that in the pool manager's argument?`,
      opts:[`To be admitted to the conservatory, a pianist must audition in person. Mira was admitted to the conservatory. So Mira must have auditioned in person.`,`To win the county baking contest, a cake must be made from scratch. Bo's cake was made from scratch, with no mixes. So Bo's cake will win.`,`To be hired at the city library, an applicant usually needs a degree. Sol has no degree. So Sol probably won't be hired at the city library this year.`,`Most people who pass the county swim test are hired at one of the pools. Anya passed the swim test. So Anya will probably be hired at one of the pools.`,`Bo's cake won the county baking contest last year. So Bo's cake must have been the best cake anyone entered in that contest last year.`],
      a:1, why:`Parallel flaw. The manager treats a requirement for being hired as if passing it guarantees a hire. The baking argument does the same with a requirement for winning. The conservatory argument reasons correctly from admission back to the requirement. The library and swim test arguments draw hedged conclusions from what usually happens, and the last one confuses winning with being best, a different mistake.`, task:`LR15`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Music critic: Every one of the fourteen songs on the Velez brothers' new album runs less than three minutes, and several of them are barely two. So the album as a whole is a quick listen, short enough to play from start to finish during a morning commute.<br><br>Which one of the following arguments is most similar in its flawed reasoning to the critic's argument?`,
      opts:[`The wall is too heavy for one person to lift. So every brick in the wall must be too heavy for one person to lift.`,`Every brick in the wall came from the Marsh yard. So the wall is made of bricks from the Marsh yard.`,`Every brick in the wall is light enough for one person to lift. So the wall is too.`,`Every brick in the wall is red, and the mortar is red as well. So the wall as a whole is red.`,`The bricks in the wall were made at a plant known for its quality. So the wall will last a long time.`],
      a:2, why:`Parallel flaw. The critic reasons that because each part is short, the whole is short, though fourteen short songs can add up to a long album. The light bricks argument makes the same move. The heavy wall argument runs from the whole to the parts, a different flaw. The Marsh yard and red wall arguments carry a trait from the parts to the whole where it really does carry over, so they aren't flawed. The plant argument is a different kind of reasoning.`, task:`LR15`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`School board member: At Grant Middle School, students who eat the free breakfast in the cafeteria score higher, on average, on first period quizzes than students who don't. Clearly the breakfast program is improving students' performance, and we should expand it to the high school next fall.<br><br>The reasoning in which one of the following is most similar to the flawed reasoning in the board member's argument?`,
      opts:[`The gardening club's president says that music helps tomatoes grow, and she has won many ribbons. So music must help tomatoes grow.`,`Gardeners who play music for their plants have bigger harvests. So gardeners with bigger harvests must be the ones who play music.`,`Tomato plants given more water grew larger in a test where every plant was otherwise treated the same. So more water helps tomatoes grow.`,`Gardeners who play music for their tomato plants get bigger harvests than gardeners who don't. So music makes tomato plants produce more.`,`Some gardeners who play music have large harvests, and some have small ones. So playing music has no effect at all on how much plants produce.`],
      a:3, why:`Parallel flaw. The board member sees two things go together, eating the breakfast and higher scores, and concludes one causes the other, ignoring other differences between the groups. The music and harvest argument does exactly that. The president argument leans on an authority. The bigger harvests argument reverses the relationship rather than reading cause into it. The water test controls for other differences, so it isn't flawed that way. The last one draws a sweeping negative from mixed results.`, task:`LR15`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Resident: The proposal to add protected bike lanes on Fairmount Avenue was written by Councilor Kettering, who owns a bicycle shop two blocks from Fairmount. She stands to profit if more people ride. So the council should reject the proposal; the lanes aren't needed.<br><br>Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to the resident's?`,
      opts:[`The study was paid for by the cereal's maker. So its findings deserve a second look from researchers who weren't paid by the company.`,`The study found that Crunchwell cereal is healthy, and most other studies of cereal have found the same. So Crunchwell cereal is healthy.`,`The study found that Crunchwell cereal is healthy. So every other cereal that Crunchwell's maker sells must be healthy as well.`,`No study has ever shown that Crunchwell cereal is unhealthy. So Crunchwell cereal must be a healthy breakfast for most people.`,`The study showing Crunchwell cereal is healthy was paid for by its maker. So the cereal isn't healthy, and the finding is false.`],
      a:4, why:`Parallel flaw. The resident rejects a proposal because of who wrote it and what she might gain, without looking at whether the lanes are needed. The last cereal argument rejects a finding the same way. Asking for a second look is reasonable, not flawed. The others make different mistakes: following the crowd, stretching one product to all, and treating no proof of harm as proof of health.`, task:`LR15`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Councilor Imai: The planned downtown parking garage will reduce traffic, because drivers will no longer circle the blocks looking for street parking.<br>Resident: Millbrook built a garage of the same size in the same kind of downtown four years ago. Within a year, traffic there had risen by a fifth, as the easier parking drew more people to drive in.<br><br>The resident responds to Councilor Imai's argument by`,
      opts:[`questioning whether Councilor Imai has any expertise in traffic planning`,`pointing to a comparable case in which the predicted effect didn't occur`,`arguing that drivers who circle the block are a small share of traffic`,`showing that two of Imai's own premises contradict one another`,`proposing a different way of reducing traffic in the downtown area`],
      a:1, why:`Method of reasoning. The resident offers Millbrook, a similar garage in a similar downtown, where traffic rose instead of falling. That's a comparable case cutting against the prediction. The resident never mentions Imai's expertise, how many drivers circle, any contradiction, or another plan.`, task:`LR16`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Bookseller: Every review of Pia Szabo's new novel calls it challenging. Challenging work is work that most people who attempt it fail to complete, which is why challenging climbs and challenging exams have such high failure rates. So most people who start Szabo's novel won't finish it.<br><br>The bookseller's reasoning is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`relies on a shift in the meaning of a key word between its premises`,`draws a conclusion from reviews that may not represent all reviews`,`takes for granted that reviewers read the entire novel before writing`,`treats the views of booksellers as more reliable than those of critics`,`assumes that readers who fail to finish a book never try it a second time`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw. The reviewers likely mean the novel is demanding or thought provoking. The bookseller switches to a sense of challenging that means most people fail at it. The argument says every review, so representativeness isn't the issue. Nothing turns on whether reviewers finished it, how booksellers rank against critics, or second tries.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Planner: The town has decided it needs a new library and has chosen the old mill site for it. Converting the mill into a library would cost less than tearing the mill down and putting up a new building there, and the converted mill would hold just as many books. So the town should convert the mill rather than tear it down.<br><br>The planner's argument depends on assuming which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`The old mill is the oldest building that's still standing anywhere in the town.`,`Most residents would rather have their library in a historic old building.`,`The converted mill won't cost so much more to run that the savings vanish.`,`A new building on the site would hold more books than the converted mill.`,`No other site in the town is large enough to hold the town's new library.`],
      a:2, why:`Necessary assumption. The argument rests on cost. Deny this choice: the converted mill costs so much more to run that the savings vanish. Then the cost reason no longer favors converting. The mill's age and residents' tastes don't matter to the reasoning. A new building holding more books contradicts a premise. The site is already chosen, so other sites don't matter.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Naturalist: In the spring of 2024, volunteers planted milkweed along the Dry Creek trail. In 2025, the number of monarch butterflies counted along the trail during the summer survey was more than double the number counted in 2024. The new milkweed must have drawn the monarchs to the trail.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the naturalist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Monarch numbers across the whole region fell sharply in the summer of 2023.`,`More people walked the Dry Creek trail in 2025 than in any year before it.`,`Volunteers also planted several kinds of wildflowers along the trail in 2024.`,`Monarch counts held about level on two nearby trails where nothing was planted.`,`The 2025 survey on the Dry Creek trail was done by a new team of counters.`],
      a:3, why:`Strengthen. If monarch counts held steady on similar trails without milkweed, the rise on Dry Creek is less likely to be a regional boom and more likely tied to what changed there. More walkers or a new team could explain more sightings without more monarchs, and the wildflowers offer another cause. A sharp drop in 2023 doesn't help, and a region bouncing back from it could even explain the jump.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`When the Marlow branch library moved its closing time from 6 P.M. to 9 P.M., the number of people entering the building each day fell slightly. Yet over the same months, the number of seats occupied at any given hour, averaged across the day, rose sharply.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,
      opts:[`The branch began counting visitors with a new electronic counter that same year.`,`Other branches in the city kept their old closing times throughout that year.`,`Most visitors to the Marlow branch live within one mile of the building.`,`The branch added two new reading rooms a few months after it changed its hours.`,`Many patrons who used to stop in twice a day now come once and stay into the evening.`],
      a:4, why:`Resolve the paradox. If people who used to make two short visits now make one long one, fewer entries can come with more seats filled. A new counter might change the entry count but says nothing about occupancy. Other branches and where patrons live don't touch either number. More rooms add seats, not people to sit in them.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`It can be inferred from the card that a visitor who wants to share a snack with the resident would most likely need to`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`bring a snack from home and leave it at the desk`,`buy the resident a separate item from the machines`,`split one package between them on the visitor's side`,`ask the staff for permission before opening anything`,`eat with the resident on the resident's side of the table`],
      a:1, why:`Inference. Vending machine items are the only things that can pass between them, and no two people may share a container, so the resident needs an item of his own from the machines. Outside food isn't allowed past the desk, splitting one package means sharing it, staff permission isn't mentioned, and each person's food stays on that person's side.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The card mentions that certain colors can be mistaken for what residents wear primarily in order to`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`give the reason behind the limit on the colors visitors wear`,`list the colors that residents are required to wear each day`,`warn visitors that staff may end a visit for any violation`,`explain why coats and hats must be left outside the room`,`suggest that visitors should dress formally for each visit`],
      a:0, why:`Function of a detail. The clause follows the list of colors and begins with "because," so it explains the rule. It doesn't say which colors residents wear, the warning about ending visits comes later and covers every rule, coats and hats are a separate rule, and nothing calls for formal dress.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the relationship between the two passages?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Passage B rejects the findings described in passage A as the product of flawed measurement.`,`Passage B offers new measurements of wild flocks that confirm the claims made in passage A.`,`Passage B accepts the findings in passage A but questions how far they extend to wild flocks.`,`Passage B explains the aerodynamic theory that passage A takes for granted without stating.`,`Passage B argues that formation flight developed for reasons that have nothing to do with energy.`],
      a:2, why:`Comparative passages. Passage B calls the studies careful and their measurements sound, then questions whether birds raised by people and led by an aircraft tell us how much wild flocks save. It doesn't reject the measurements or add new ones. Passage A states the theory itself. Passage B says other functions don't contradict the savings, so "nothing to do with energy" goes far past it.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The authors of both passages would most likely agree that`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`wild flocks save as much energy in formation as the trained birds did`,`formation flight is easier to study in birds trained to follow people`,`formation flight probably does little to save energy for most birds`,`the V shape mainly helps young birds learn routes from older birds`,`the pelican study was more reliable than the ibis study that followed it`],
      a:1, why:`Comparative passages. Passage A says nobody can fit a wild flock with instruments and follow it, which is why the studies used trained birds. Passage B says those birds were chosen because researchers can fit and follow them. Passage B doubts the first choice, neither author doubts the savings, only passage B mentions young birds following others, and neither ranks the two studies.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`The concern raised in passage B about the two studies is most analogous to which one of the following?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`A medicine is tested only on patients who take it under close watch in a clinic, and doctors wonder how well it works at home.`,`A medicine that worked well in clinic trials is later found to cause harm, and it's withdrawn from sale at once.`,`A medicine is tested on many kinds of patients in many settings over several years before it's finally approved for use.`,`A doctor prescribes a new medicine without any testing at all, relying only on how similar medicines have worked.`,`Two clinics test the same medicine with the same methods and report results that contradict each other entirely.`],
      a:0, why:`Analogy. Passage B accepts the results but notes that the birds were studied in special, controlled conditions and asks how far the results carry to ordinary ones. The clinic medicine matches. Harm, testing in many settings, no testing, and contradictory results are different relationships.`, task:`RC10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The method the 2001 study described in passage A used to gauge the pelicans' effort is most similar to which one of the following?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`A coach judges her swimmers only by their race times and ignores how tired they say they feel.`,`A coach asks her swimmers to rate their own effort on a scale after each practice session.`,`A coach checks her swimmers' pulse after each lap to see what the lap cost them.`,`A coach times her swimmers in a pool to predict how they'll do later in open water.`,`A coach watches video of her swimmers' strokes to find faults in their technique.`],
      a:2, why:`Analogy. The pelican researchers measured effort by heart rate, a bodily signal, rather than by speed or the birds' output. Checking a swimmer's pulse is the same kind of measure. Race times are output, self ratings are reports, and video is about form. Timing in a pool to predict open water matches passage B's worry, not the 2001 method.`, task:`RC10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She sat in the car with the engine off and her phone in her lap, and she didn't start it.`,
    `Some neighbor kid, mija. That was March, on the phone. Some kid. Nobody. That was at the table, with his coffee in his hand. Nobody, mija. Let it be nobody. That was twenty minutes ago.`,
    `Three times he'd put the kid down the way Ana put down a conclusion, at the end, where everything else was supposed to hold it up. Except nothing held it up. A man doesn't swing first for nobody. Not him. Not the man who used to carry her report cards around in his wallet and show them to the whole bar.`,
    `And the bishop. He'd taken her knight with it in January and left it sitting there, something easy to take. Today, with the whole board in front of her, she'd seen what the easy take would open, and she hadn't made it. And he'd put the punch down on the table the same way, and she'd taken it, and kept on taking it, and quit looking at the rest of the board. She'd seen it on the board. She hadn't seen it at the table.`,
    `She called the taqueria. Whoever picked up yelled for Ronnie without covering the phone. It went down on the counter. A fryer, a radio in back, somebody arguing about a delivery. Then Ronnie, saying her name. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Dani."</span>`,
    `She asked if there'd been a kid in the bar that night.`,
    `It took him a while. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"A kid. On a Series night."</span> Then, slower: <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I told you. I couldn't see over the people. That guy was yelling down at something, by the hallway."</span> Another wait. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Down."</span> The fryer again. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Your dad, every time he came up, I poured a beer and a Sprite. Later on just the Sprite."</span>`,
    `Who was the Sprite for, she asked. He'd figured one of Luis's guys was driving, he said. Then nothing, just him breathing on the line. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I never once thought about who it was for,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Not once, Dani."</span>`,
    `She thanked him. She didn't remember hanging up.`,
    `The prep book was in the glove box, and the envelope was still in it, marking her page. MAYBE HE DID IT across the top, in her own capitals. NEEDED. ENOUGH. HE SWUNG FIRST. Since December she'd only ever written on that one side.`,
    `She turned it over. Nothing on the back. She wrote on it.`,
    `WHO WAS THE KID?`,
    `Her phone buzzed in her lap. Teo.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="3:52 PM">how is he</prop>`,
    ((s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)
      ?`After the carport she hadn't expected to hear from him for a while. He hadn't asked about their father in ten years.`
      :`He hadn't asked about their father in ten years.`),
    `She typed one word and looked at it a while. Then two more lines, because the last one was true and it was Teo's more than hers, and she sent it before she could fix anything.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Dani" time="3:55 PM">tired. he asked about you. he said you always took with the wrong pawn</prop>`,
    `Three dots came up. They went away. They came back for a second, and went away again, and stayed gone.`,
    `She laid the envelope on the dash with the new side down, so the old side looked up at her, MAYBE HE DID IT in her own capitals, and backed out. Past the fences, out to 61, then 76, two hours with the sun in her eyes most of the way. It rode like that the whole way home. She knew what was on the other side.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch07.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Counting Jackets`
});
